V1 Roadmap

V1A: End-to-end non-rhythmic text tab
Goal: prove the full product loop. A user records a short guitar phrase in the browser, previews it, uploads it, the
backend stores it, processes it through Basic Pitch, infers simple string/fret positions, generates a text tab, and
shows/downloads the result.

001-audio-analyser-basic-pitch.md
Python audio-analyser service. Wrap Spotify Basic Pitch, read prepared WAV audio from S3-compatible storage, and return
normalised timestamped note events. This proves the riskiest technical assumption early: whether Basic Pitch gives
useful note data for short guitar recordings.

002-audio-analyser-openapi-contract.md
Defines the work to create the OpenAPI contract for the audio-analyser service. The actual contract should live with the
Python service and describe GET /health and POST /v1/analyse-audio so the Spring backend has a stable API to call.

003-backend-recording-session-upload.md
Spring backend endpoint for creating a RecordingSession and uploading original browser media. It validates metadata,
assigns the local/dev ownerUserId, stores the original recording in S3/LocalStack, creates a MediaAsset, and returns
the new session details. No processing happens yet.

004-backend-recording-session-status.md
Spring backend endpoint for reading the current RecordingSession status. Initially this just lets the frontend retrieve
uploaded session metadata and media summary. Later this same endpoint can expose processing progress, failure state,
and generated exports.

005-frontend-recording-preview-upload.md
First browser recording UI. The user can record a short clip, stop, preview it, discard/re-record, and upload it to the
backend. This should support mobile and desktop browsers, with simple guidance like clean tone, avoid distortion, and
keep recordings short.

006-frontend-metronome-basic.md
Adds optional metronome support to the recording UI. The user can enter BPM and enable/disable a click while recording.
This does not mean rhythmic tab is implemented yet; BPM is captured as useful metadata and a recording aid.

007-backend-processing-job-pipeline.md
Introduces ProcessingJob records and the simple database-backed worker inside the Spring backend. Jobs are created
sequentially and move through PENDING, PROCESSING, COMPLETED, or FAILED. This creates the pipeline structure without
yet implementing FFmpeg, audio analysis, tab inference, or export generation.

008-backend-media-preparation-ffmpeg.md
Adds media preparation in the Spring backend worker. For audio/video recordings, FFmpeg extracts a prepared WAV audio
asset from the original combined media. The prepared audio is stored in S3 and recorded as a derived MediaAsset.

009-backend-audio-analyser-client.md
Adds the Spring HTTP client for calling the Python audio-analyser service using its OpenAPI contract. This maps
request/response models, handles timeouts and errors, and keeps the analyser integration behind a clean backend
interface.

010-backend-audio-analysis-job.md
Wires the audio-analyser client into the ProcessingJob pipeline. The AUDIO_ANALYSIS job finds the prepared audio
MediaAsset, calls the Python service, stores the returned analysis JSON in S3, creates an AnalysisResult record, and
then creates the next job.

011-tab-document-json-schema-v1.md
Defines the first internal TabDocument JSON shape. This is Riffle’s neutral internal tab model before export. For V1A
it should be six-string standard tuning, non-rhythmic, and contain tab notes with string, fret, source pitch, timing
metadata, and confidence.

012-tab-inference-v1.md
Implements the first simple TabInferenceEngine. It reads the audio AnalysisResult, converts detected MIDI pitches into
possible string/fret positions, chooses a basic playable position, and writes a TabDocument JSON artefact to S3. This
is intentionally simple and not yet rhythm-aware or video-aware.

013-text-tab-export-v1.md
Generates a basic text tab from the TabDocument. The backend writes tab.txt to S3, creates a GeneratedExport record,
and marks the session complete. This is the first user-visible tab output.

014-frontend-processing-status.md
Adds frontend polling and status display. After upload, the UI polls the backend status endpoint and shows simple
states like uploaded, processing, completed, or failed. This gives the user feedback while the backend worker runs.

015-frontend-text-tab-result.md
Shows the generated text tab in the frontend and allows the user to download it. This completes the first end-to-end
loop: record, upload, process, generate tab, display/download result.

V1B: Make the first version usable and stable
Goal: turn the basic end-to-end flow into something nicer to use locally and in a private test environment.

016-local-dev-compose.md
Defines the local development setup. This should cover Postgres, LocalStack, audio-analyser, backend, frontend, and any
required environment variables. The goal is that the whole system can be started predictably without manually wiring
everything each time.

017-error-handling-and-user-messages.md
Standardises backend/frontend error handling for the first end-to-end flow. This should define safe user-facing
messages for upload failure, processing failure, missing session, failed audio analysis, and export failure. It should
avoid leaking stack traces or internal storage details.

018-private-aws-test-deployment.md
Defines a private AWS deployment for controlled testing. This is not public production. It should cover S3, RDS/Aurora
Postgres, ECS Fargate services, frontend hosting, basic logging, and temporary private access protection.

019-observability-basic.md
Adds basic logs and operational visibility. The goal is to trace one recording session through upload, media
preparation, audio analysis, tab inference, and export. This should be lightweight: useful logs, correlation
IDs/session IDs, and simple health checks.

V1C: Rhythm and better musical output
Goal: move beyond “notes on strings” into more useful notation.

020-rhythmic-tab-output-mode.md
Introduces RHYTHMIC_TAB as a real output mode. BPM becomes required, and the backend starts mapping note timings onto a
beat grid. This should cover quantisation settings, beat/bar positions, note durations, and how to handle imperfect
human timing.

021-quantisation-v1.md
Implements the first quantisation algorithm. It takes note start/duration times and BPM, then snaps them to a simple
grid. This should start conservative and transparent, because bad quantisation can make a tab worse than no rhythm.

022-musicxml-export.md
Adds MusicXML export from the TabDocument. This is the first proper editable notation-style export format and is likely
more useful than PDF for external tools. It should build on the internal TabDocument rather than redoing inference
logic.

023-pdf-export-basic.md
Adds a readable PDF export. This is useful for teachers and sharing exercises, but it is less useful for editing than
MusicXML. The PDF can initially be simple and generated from the existing tab/export model.

V1D: Product readiness before public launch
Goal: make Riffle safe enough to expose beyond private testing.

024-authentication-and-ownership.md
Adds real authentication and replaces the local/dev ownerUserId with the authenticated user identity. This must add
ownership checks so users can only access their own sessions, media, tab documents, and exports.

025-usage-limits.md
Adds usage limits before billing. This should define limits such as maximum recording duration, number of saved
sessions, processing limits, and storage limits. It protects cost and abuse before the app is public.

026-billing-and-subscriptions.md
Adds paid tiers, probably via Stripe. This should define what free users get, what paid users get, and how usage limits
change by plan. It should come after the core product is proven.

027-retention-and-delete-session.md
Defines retention and deletion behaviour. Users should be able to delete sessions, and the system should know what
happens to original media, prepared media, analysis JSON, tab documents, and generated exports. This matters because
recordings may contain personal audio/video.

028-production-hardening.md
Covers rate limiting, upload size limits, monitoring, alerting, safer error handling, deployment checks, and basic
security review. This is the final gate before a public release.

V2: Video-assisted tab inference
Goal: add the differentiator: use video to improve string/fret and technique inference.

029-frontend-audio-video-capture-and-calibration.md
Improves the recording UI for AUDIO_VIDEO mode. The user can align the guitar neck in frame, record combined
audio/video, and provide simple calibration metadata. This is where the UI starts supporting the future video
differentiator properly.

030-video-analyser-openapi-contract.md
Defines the OpenAPI contract for the future Python video-analyser service. It should return timestamped
isual/performance hints, not tabs.

031-video-analyser-v1.md
Implements the first video-analyser service. It likely uses OpenCV and MediaPipe to detect hands/landmarks and returns
rough timestamped hints. It does not generate tabs or choose final string/fret positions.

032-video-analysis-job.md
Adds VIDEO_ANALYSIS into the backend ProcessingJob pipeline. The backend calls the video-analyser, stores Video
AnalysisResult JSON in S3, and makes it available to tab inference.

033-video-aware-tab-inference.md
Updates TabInferenceEngine so it can combine audio note events with video hints. The goal is to improve decisions like
where an E4 was played and eventually help detect slides, hammer-ons, pull-offs, and bends.

V3: Proper editor and richer exports
Goal: make Riffle feel like a real product rather than only a generator.

034-tab-preview-ui.md
Adds a better frontend tab preview rather than plain text display. It should render the internal TabDocument in a
readable way, but still avoid becoming a full editor too early.

035-tab-correction-ui.md
Allows the user to correct generated string/fret choices. This is important because AI transcription will not be
perfect. Corrections may later feed improved exports or model evaluation.

036-user-edited-tab-documents.md
Adds the concept of user-edited TabDocuments. This lets the system preserve the original generated tab and the
user-corrected version separately.

037-guitar-pro-compatible-export.md
Investigates or implements Guitar Pro-compatible export. This should come after MusicXML and after the internal
TabDocument has stabilised.

